Original commit body from f74c23f5c4ec010511db4e50838b2698e5b76d64:

Four of this ADR's own claims did not survive W1/W2/W3, and the ADR now says
so in place rather than leaving the next reader to rediscover them.

1. ITS NAMED ACCEPTANCE TEST DOES NOT TEST WHAT IT SAYS, and run literally it
   would have DEPRECATED a value that is firing. aardvark-dns is Rust-only
   (16 .rs, zero .js), so the dependents are activation-gated off and produce
   no AnalysisRun at all; and removing tree-sitter-language-pack does not
   remove rust, which loads from a dedicated wheel. Corrected form recorded.

2. Pass.requires names the wrong distribution for 25 of 26 grammar-gated
   literals, which is what aimed that test at the wrong package. Nothing but a
   human reads the field, so nothing catches it. -> WI-pakof.

3. W2 was sized at "~4 sites, two f-string templates". It is 49 sites across
   38 files; the f-string is copy-pasted into thirty analyzers.

4. pass_crashed is not speculative. The zero-instance count measured the
   free-text twin, not the state: six analyzer sites catch a parser
   CONSTRUCTOR exception, which is the same state under a different container.

Also records the two things the implementation established that the ADR could
not have: its own re-decide criterion for declaration repair is MET (W1 found
261 of 893 surveys carrying a falsified declaration), and W3 needed a rule the
ADR did not state -- the producer fills only a field the pass BODY left empty,
because a bridge linker's true no_candidate_construct claim about the side it
could scan must not be overruled by a plausible one.

Verdict table and the three stale in-body claims re-pointed at the corrections
section, per the ADR-lifecycle rule against leaving retracted text standing
beside the ruling that replaced it.

